<p>To prepare your team for a brand video shoot, start with the story you want to tell, then pick who will be on camera and walk each person through exactly what will happen: the questions they'll be asked, what to wear, where to be and when. Give them talking points instead of a script, let them practice out loud, and build a shoot day schedule that keeps the rest of the business running while you film.</p>

<p>Most of the work happens before the camera shows up. A team that knows the plan relaxes faster, gives better answers and gets back to work sooner. Here's how to get everyone ready, whether you're a shop in Albuquerque, a school in Rio Rancho or a nonprofit anywhere in New Mexico.</p>

<h2>Start with the story, then decide who tells it</h2>

<p>Before you book a single person, agree on the one thing you want viewers to feel and remember. Everything else, from who's on camera to which rooms you film, follows from that. If you're also planning how the footage will be used afterward, our post on <a href="/blog/brand-film-fundraising-social-onboarding">planning one brand film for fundraising, social and onboarding</a> covers that side.</p>

<p>Then choose your on camera people on purpose:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Pick people who can speak to the story,</strong> not only the most senior titles. A front desk lead or a longtime customer often says more than an executive reading a mission statement.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep the speaking list short.</strong> Two to four interviews is plenty for most brand videos. More voices usually means a thinner story.</li>
  <li><strong>Ask, don't assign.</strong> Some people love the camera and some really don't. Plenty of team members are happy to appear in b-roll (working, laughing, helping a customer) without sitting for an interview.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Prepare interviews with talking points, not scripts</h2>

<p>Memorized lines almost always sound stiff. What reads well on paper rarely sounds natural out loud, and people tend to freeze when they lose their place. Talking points let people answer like themselves.</p>

<h3>What to send each speaker</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>The questions they'll be asked,</strong> a few days ahead. No surprises.</li>
  <li><strong>Three or four talking points</strong> they can hit in their own words.</li>
  <li><strong>A note that mistakes don't matter.</strong> They can stop, restart or rephrase. That's what editing is for.</li>
  <li><strong>The basics:</strong> where to show up, what time and roughly how long their part will take.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Practice out loud</h3>

<p>Ask each speaker to answer the questions out loud once or twice before the shoot, to a coworker or into their phone. They don't need to rehearse until it's polished. They just need to have heard themselves say it. Remind them to answer in full sentences that include the question ("What I love about working here is..."), so each answer makes sense on its own without the interviewer's voice.</p>

<h2>Wardrobe and grooming that look good on camera</h2>

<p>Clothes cause more last minute problems than almost anything else, so settle them early.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Wear solid colors</strong> that suit your brand. Medium tones usually film better than bright white or pure black.</li>
  <li><strong>Skip small stripes, tight checks and busy patterns.</strong> They can shimmer or flicker on camera.</li>
  <li><strong>Avoid other companies' logos</strong> and slogans on shirts and hats. Branded shirts or uniforms of your own are great if that's how customers see your team.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose comfort.</strong> People should be able to sit, stand and move naturally for a couple of hours.</li>
  <li><strong>Bring a backup top.</strong> If a color clashes with the background or blends into it, you can swap it in a minute.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep it everyday.</strong> Hair, makeup and accessories should look like a good version of a normal workday, so the video matches what customers will see in person.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Get the space ready before shoot day</h2>

<p>Walk through every location you plan to film a few days ahead, ideally with whoever is running the camera.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Clear the clutter.</strong> Stacks of paper, cords, personal items and old signage show up more on video than in real life.</li>
  <li><strong>Find a quiet room for interviews.</strong> Listen for buzzing lights, air conditioning, traffic and phones. Plan to silence what you can.</li>
  <li><strong>Think about light.</strong> Window light is lovely but changes through the day, and harsh midday sun can be tough outdoors. Schedule outdoor shots for softer morning or late afternoon light when you can, and have a plan for wind on breezy days.</li>
  <li><strong>Check signs and screens.</strong> Make sure anything visible is current, spelled right and on brand.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Build a shoot day schedule your team can follow</h2>

<p>A clear schedule is the single best thing you can hand your team. Share it a few days ahead and again the morning of.</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Setup time.</strong> Give the crew time to set lights and sound before anyone is needed.</li>
  <li><strong>Interviews early.</strong> People are freshest in the morning, before the day wears them out.</li>
  <li><strong>B-roll in blocks.</strong> Group action shots by location so the crew isn't hauling gear back and forth.</li>
  <li><strong>Real moments on their natural schedule.</strong> If the best moment happens at lunch rush or during a class change, plan around it rather than staging it.</li>
  <li><strong>Buffer time.</strong> Something always takes longer than planned. A little slack keeps the day calm.</li>
</ol>

<p>Name one point person on your side who knows the schedule, can find people and can make quick calls. It keeps the crew from interrupting everyone else with questions.</p>

<h2>Help nervous people relax on camera</h2>

<p>Almost everyone is a little nervous. A few small things make a big difference:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Keep the room small.</strong> Only the people who need to be there. An audience makes anyone tense.</li>
  <li><strong>Start with an easy warm up question,</strong> like how long they've worked there or what a normal day looks like.</li>
  <li><strong>Talk to the interviewer, not the lens,</strong> unless the plan calls for direct to camera.</li>
  <li><strong>Encourage real reactions.</strong> A laugh or a pause to think often makes the best moment in the final cut.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Sort out permissions and releases</h2>

<p>Get a signed video release from anyone who will clearly appear on camera, including customers or volunteers who join in. If you film in a public place or someone else's property, check whether you need permission first. Schools and youth programs need to confirm which students have photo and video permission well before the shoot day, so no one has to be pulled out of a shot at the last minute.</p>

<h2>Protect the rest of the business</h2>

<p>The shoot shouldn't stop the business. Let customers know if anything will be different that day, cover shifts for people who are on camera, and keep filming out of the way of anyone who isn't part of it. When the team doesn't feel like the shoot is taking over their day, they show up on camera more relaxed.</p>

<h2>A quick checklist for your team</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Story and key message agreed</li>
  <li>On camera speakers chosen and willing</li>
  <li>Questions and talking points sent</li>
  <li>Wardrobe guidance sent, backup tops packed</li>
  <li>Locations walked, cleaned and quiet</li>
  <li>Schedule shared, point person named</li>
  <li>Releases signed and permissions confirmed</li>
  <li>Shifts covered and customers told</li>
</ul>

<p>If you'd also like still photos from the same day, plan for them now. Real images of your team are worth a lot on your website, and our post on <a href="/blog/real-photos-vs-stock-photos-website">why real photos beat stock photos</a> explains why.</p>

<p>For a sense of what good preparation makes possible, DYAD produced <a href="/work/chesterton-academy">Chesterton Academy of Albuquerque's five minute brand film</a> in two weeks. If you're planning a brand video and want help with the story, the shoot and the edit, take a look at our <a href="/video-photo-production">video and photo production services in Albuquerque</a>.</p>
